  "id": "Compound::DB00582 - Gene::1557",
  "verbalization": "Voriconazole is linked to Cyp2c19 through a series of molecular interactions that involve the enzyme cytochrome P450 2C19. The evidence suggests that Voriconazole may act as a substrate or inhibitor of Cyp2c19, as it causes the development of diabetes insipidus, duodenal ulcer perforation, and oculogyration, all of which are associated with the enzyme's activity. These conditions are mediated by the binding of Voriconazole to specific compounds, such as fosphenytoin and aprepitant, which in turn bind to Cyp2c19. The lowest common ancestor of these pathways is the class of compounds known as organonitrogen heterocyclic compounds, indicating a shared biochemical mechanism. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential role for Voriconazole in modulating Cyp2c19 activity, possibly through the interaction with these compounds, which may explain the observed effects on enzyme function.",
